</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant disorder that results in the progressive destruction of brain cells. Key characteristics include:
- **Genetic Basis**: It is caused by a dominant allele, meaning that if a parent has the disease, there is a 50% chance their offspring will inherit it (unless the parent is homozygous dominant, in which case all offspring would inherit the disease).
- **Symptoms**: Progressive destruction of brain cells leads to severe neurological decline.
- **Age of Onset**: The disease typically manifests after age 30, though early onset cases in individuals in their twenties have been reported.

This disorder is rare among autosomal dominants and is notable for its delayed onset, which often occurs after affected individuals have already had children.
