","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function $f$ at the point $(a, f(a))$ is given by the point-point form of a line, where the point is $(a, f(a))$ and the slope is the derivative of $f$ at $a$, which is $f'(a)$.

The point-point form of a line is given by:
$$y - y_1 = m(x - x_0)$$
where $(x_0, y_1)$ is a point on the line and $m$ is the slope of the line.

In this case, we have the point $(a, f(a))$ and the slope $f'(a)$. So, $x_0 = a$, $y_1 = f(a)$, and $m = f'(a)$.
Substituting these into the point-point form, we get:
$$y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)$$

This is the equation of the tangent line to the graph of $f$ at the point $(a, f(a))$.

We can rewrite this equation in the slope-intercept form:
$$y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)$$
$$y = f'(a)x - a f'(a) + f(a)$$

