<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the activation energy \( E_a \) for the reaction \( H_2 + I_2 \rightarrow 2HI \) using the Arrhenius equation. Given the forward rate constants \( k_{f1} = 10^{-1} \) at \( 1/T_1 = 0.0018 \, \text{K}^{-1} \) and \( k_{f2} = 10^4 \) at \( 1/T_2 = 0.0012 \, \text{K}^{-1} \), and the gas constant \( R = 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The Arrhenius equation in logarithmic form is:
\[
\ln \left( \frac{k_{f2}}{k_{f1}} \right) = -\frac{E_a}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_2} - \frac{1}{T_1} \right).
\]
Substitute the given values:
\[
\ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right) = \ln(10^5) = 5 \ln(10) \approx 11.513,
\]
\[
\frac{1}{T_2} - \frac{1}{T_1} = 0.0012 - 0.0018 = -0.0006 \, \text{K}^{-1}.
\]
Rearranging the Arrhenius equation to solve for \( E_a \):
\[
E_a = -R \left( \frac{\ln(k_{f2}/k_{f1})}{1/T_2 - 1/T_1} \right) = -2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1} \left( \frac{11.513}{-0.0006} \right).
\]
Calculating \( E_a \):
\[
E_a \approx 2 \times \frac{11.513}{0.0006} \approx 38,377 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1} \approx 38 \, \text{kcal mol}^{-1}.
\]
Thus, the activation energy is:
\[
\boxed{38 \, \text{kcal mol}^{-1}}.
\]
</GROUND_TRUTH>

