     - (b) Enthalpy: $\boxed{48.2 \text{ kJ/kg dry air}}$  
     - (c) Relative humidity: $\boxed{54.1\%}$  
     - (d) Dew-point temperature: $\boxed{12.3°\text{C}}$  
     - (e) Specific volume: $\boxed{0.85 \text{ m}^3/\text{kg dry air}}$  

   - At 3000 m (~70 kPa):  
     - (a) Specific humidity: $\boxed{0.0128 \text{ kg H}_2\text{O/kg dry air}}$  
     - (b) Enthalpy: $\boxed{48.2 \text{ kJ/kg dry air}}$ (unchanged, as enthalpy is independent of pressure for ideal gases)  
     - (c) Relative humidity: $\boxed{54.1\%}$ (unchanged, as relative humidity is a ratio and not directly pressure-dependent)  
     - (d) Dew-point temperature: $\boxed{12.3°\text{C}}$ (unchanged, as dew-point depends on vapor pressure, not total pressure)  
     - (e) Specific volume: $\boxed{1.22 \text{ m}^3/\text{kg dry air}}$ (increases due to lower pressure)  

Note: The specific humidity increases at higher altitudes because the same mass of water vapor is now distributed in a larger volume of dry air (due to lower pressure). The other properties remain unchanged or adjust accordingly.
